<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Khuttal</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 751 CE (approx.) · capital Hulbuk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A mountain principality north of the Amu Darya, famed for its horses, whose kings resisted the Arab conquest for decades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Khuttal lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakhsh-river">Vakhsh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panj-river">Panj</a> rivers in what is now southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. It emerged in the 6th and 7th centuries as one of the principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a> under Hephthalite and then Turkic overlordship, ruled by kings with the title Khuttalan-shah, and its capital is identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulbuk">Hulbuk</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Khuttal was a land of valleys and mountain pastures known for horses and for its warrior aristocracy. Its rulers bore Turkic or Hephthalite titles and maintained ties with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yabghus-of-tokharistan">Yabghus of Tokharistan</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turgesh-khaganate">Turgesh Khaganate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>. The king al-Sabal fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad</a> governors in the 720s and 730s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Khuttal accepted Abbasid suzerainty after the mid-8th century, and its local dynasty continued as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> for a time. It later became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a>, and the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulbuk">Hulbuk</a> flourished under its Islamic-era governors.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-khuttal.jpg" alt="Principality of Khuttal" width="320" height="155"><figcaption>Image: Cplakidas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transoxiana_8th_century.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 751 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hulbuk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>al-Sabal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Zoroastrianism, Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bactrian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Tajikistan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khuttal, Khuttalan</dd></dl></aside></div>

